    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d433">

                <pb facs="0550" n="482" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg482"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.857 Ar 1/103½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic"> Colonel E. M. House to President Wilson </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New York</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-09-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 26, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Governor</hi>: Bernstorff came to see me very
                    promptly this morning. He said he had an intimation from Washington, indirectly
                    through Tumulty, that you wanted him to hasten the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Arabic</hi> matter and to see Lansing when he passes through New York
                    before he reaches Washington.</p>
                <p>This is what he would like to know.</p>
                <p>
                    <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>Do you want anything done before he sees Lansing? He reiterates that
                            what he can do now is to give additional assurances directly from his
                            government that no passenger vessel will be torpedoed in the future
                            without notice, and that the ship will have the benefit of the
                            doubt.</item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item> They are willing to submit the <hi rendition="#italic">Arabic</hi>
                            case to an international commission and accept the principle of
                            indemnity for the loss of life.</item>
                        <label/>
                        <item>If this is not satisfactory, he will take the question up with his
                            government as to some form of disavowal based upon American evidence.
                            But if his government makes the disavowal they would want to refer the
                            question of reparation to the Hague. </item>
                        <label>(3)</label>
                        <item>If you decide a disavowal must be made, do you want him to make an
                            effort at once, or would you prefer that he wait until after he consults
                            Lansing?</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>I have told him I thought I could get him this information by Tuesday
                        morning.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d433-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Your devoted,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">E. M. House</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
